--- log ---
2026-09-14T12:19Z claude created
2026-09-14T12:19Z claude note Found by the author, listening, during ticket 0769's Orca pass. He put it exactly: "On click to turn on, it says Turn indexing off because the button just changed to off. That is confusing." Neither the AT-SPI read nor the speech log would have caught this — both show a correct name for a correct control. What is wrong is what a person concludes from hearing it at that moment, which is why the pass needed a listener and not more instrumentation.

--- body ---
## Context

The panel's switch is a button whose accessible name IS its action: "Turn
indexing off" while indexing is on, "Turn indexing on" while it is off. That
reads well on screen, where the button sits beside a sentence stating the
current state.

Heard, it inverts. Click the button to turn indexing ON and the label
immediately becomes "Turn indexing off" — so Orca says **"Turn indexing off
push button"** at the exact moment indexing was turned on. A listener has no
sentence beside it and reasonably concludes the opposite of what happened.

Captured in the same session, in this order, from one click that turned
indexing on:

    SPEECH OUTPUT: 'Turn indexing off push button.'

## Why the live region does not save it today

It would have. `announceSDTTransition` writes "Indexing is on…" to
`sdt-announcer` after the settle, which corrects the impression a second later.
That is the mitigation the design already has — and ticket 0789 establishes it
does not fire on the switch-OFF transition, so in that direction the misleading
label is the ONLY thing a listener gets.

The two tickets are worth reading together, and 0789 should land first: if the
region is reliable, this one is a polish; if it is not, this one is the whole
experience.

## The author's proposal

> it should say "Indexing has been turned on, re-click to turn off"

That is a state-then-action sentence, and it is the right shape: it says what
happened before what to do next.

The conventional alternative is a toggle button — a STABLE name ("Indexing")
plus `aria-pressed`, which a screen reader renders as "Indexing, pressed" /
"Indexing, not pressed". It is less wordy and needs no live region to be
correct at the moment of the click. It also changes what a sighted user sees,
since the visible label would no longer name the action, so it is not a free
swap.

Deciding between them is the work of this ticket and it belongs to the author:
both are defensible, and one of them changes the visible UI.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — the switch's label composition and its
  `aria-label`; `describeSDTSwitchKind` for the state the sentence would name
- `tests/sdt_sitter_dialog.mjs` — pins the switch row's text today
- `verification/SDT-SITTER-UI-PANEL.md` — where the Orca reading is recorded

## Test

Whatever the remedy, the assertion is about what is announced AT THE MOMENT of
the click, not about the resting label. A test that reads the button after the
dust settles passes today and proves nothing.

## Verification

- [ ] A listener hears, on one click, a sentence that names the state that now
      holds — not the action that would undo it
- [ ] Re-measured with Orca in a real window, quoted in the UI panel record

## Invariants

- Whatever the button says, the visible panel must still tell a sighted user
  the current state; that sentence is what makes today's label acceptable on
  screen.
- No regression in the accessible name being free of the spinner glyph (0686).

## Exit criteria

Clicking the switch announces the state it produced. Recorded from a real
screen reader, not inferred from the markup.
